From brandon at brandoninvergo.com  Wed Apr  7 16:56:56 2010
From: brandon at brandoninvergo.com (Brandon Invergo)
Date: Wed, 07 Apr 2010 09:56:56 -0700
Subject: Re: [Rpy] export a package of R in python program
In-Reply-To: <k2y402ef4df1004070926gb11bfd63s8945e0e29673d5eb@mail.gmail.com>
References: <k2y402ef4df1004070926gb11bfd63s8945e0e29673d5eb@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <211255.50747.qm@web82102.mail.mud.yahoo.com>

There's no way of specifying versions in R's library() function, so I'm assuming that if you successfully have both versions of the package installed in R, they must have different names (I'm pretty sure installing a new version of a package overwrites the previous version). If that's the case, using the latest version of rpy2 (2.1.0-rc):

from rpy2.robjects.packages import importr
new_package = importr("new.package.name")
old_package = importr("old.package.name")

And then just access the functions using standard python library syntax:  old_package.old_function() or new_package.some_other_function()

If you've somehow managed to have two different versions of the same package installed in R with the same name, you'll have to find a more clever solution.

Cheers,
Brandon




